"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][11]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M11-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M11-L1 – Nearest neighbours and distance-based classification<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Bachelor: Classical learning algorithms (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L4<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate distance-based\npredictions, explain the effect of scaling and choose k\nappropriately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cases rather than parameter estimation<\/h2>\n<p>A test bench records two dimensionless measurement features for each\ncomponent. For an unknown component, a class is to be inferred from\nspecimens already tested. The k-nearest neighbours method (k-NN) finds\nthe k most similar training cases and uses their target values. Unlike a\nlinear model, it does not estimate a fixed global decision line. The\ntraining set remains essential for prediction; consequently, memory\nrequirements and search time can become substantial.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distances and majority decision<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001652_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Measurement points around (2,2); two A and one B lie within the illustrated neighbourhood radius.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Measurement points around (2,2); two A\nand one B lie within the illustrated neighbourhood radius.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Classification with three nearest neighbours.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnumerical features, the Euclidean distance is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>d<\/mi><\/munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(x,z)=\\sqrt{\\sum_{j=1}^d(x_j-z_j)^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With uniform weighting, class\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreceives the proportion\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><\/mfrac><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat p_c(x)=\\frac1k\\sum_{i\\in N_k(x)}\\mathbf1(y_i=c)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_k(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the set of the k selected indices; the indicator equals one if the\nclasses match. The highest frequency determines the prediction. A fixed\nrule must apply in the event of a tie. These proportions are not\nautomatically calibrated probabilities. For regression, the target\nvalues can be averaged instead.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Training profile<\/th>\n<th>Class<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Distance to (2,2)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>(1,2)<\/td>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(2,3)<\/td>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(3,3)<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(5,2)<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(4,4)<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>8<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The illustration marks the three nearest neighbours. With k=3, A is\npredicted with a proportion of 2/3; with k=5, B is predicted with 3/5\ninstead.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hyperparameters and computational cost<\/h2>\n<p>k=1 can be highly sensitive to outliers; larger neighbourhoods smooth\nlocal differences but may overlook small groups. A feature measured in\nmillimetres and another in micrometres would dominate the distance to\ndifferent degrees depending on the numerical scale used. Suitable\ntransformations, such as standardisation, are therefore fitted\nexclusively to training data. Categorical data require a meaningful\nencoding or distance measure. In high dimensions, proximity often\nbecomes less informative. An exhaustive search requires approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(nd)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomputational operations per query for n training points; search indices\nare particularly helpful with suitable geometry.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.neighbors <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> KNeighborsClassifier<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>]]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> KNeighborsClassifier(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>).fit(X,y).predict([[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]])[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> KNeighborsClassifier(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>).fit(X,y).predict([[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]])[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The small sample illustrates the rule, not generalisation\nperformance. The validation methods from M10 are used for that\npurpose.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>For k=3, determine the proportion for A when votes are weighted by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSolution: A receives 2, while B receives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe normalised proportion for A is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.739<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/(2+1/\\sqrt2)\\approx0.739<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA distance of zero requires a separate rule instead of division by\nzero.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/neighbors.html\">scikit-learn:\nNearest Neighbors<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/\">James et al.: An\nIntroduction to Statistical Learning with Applications in Python\n(2023)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M11-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M11-L2 – Naive Bayes and probabilistic classification<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Bachelor: Classical learning algorithms (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate class probabilities\nfrom prior and likelihood and assess the independence assumption.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence from several observations<\/h2>\n<p>A maintenance service assigns reports either to the fault class S or\nthe normal operation class N. Two binary features indicate whether a\ntemperature alert T and a noise alert G are present. The prior class\nfrequency is combined with the probability of the observations under\neach class. Bayes’ theorem from M4 gives, for a feature vector x,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/munder><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(c\\mid x)=\\frac{P(c)P(x\\mid c)}{\\sum_{a}P(a)P(x\\mid a)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Conditional independence as a modelling assumption<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001653_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two classes with prior, conditional probabilities and unnormalised mass; normalisation gives 6/7 for a fault.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two classes with prior, conditional\nprobabilities and unnormalised mass; normalisation gives 6/7 for a\nfault.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Naive Bayes: from alerts to class probability.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Naive Bayes replaces the joint feature distribution with a product of\nthe individual conditional distributions:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∏<\/mo><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>d<\/mi><\/munderover><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(x\\mid c)=\\prod_{j=1}^dP(x_j\\mid c).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This simplification assumes independence given the class, not\nnecessarily in the population as a whole. In practice, measurement\nfeatures may be strongly associated; the model can still yield useful\ndecisions, but its probabilities may be overconfident.<\/p>\n<p>For the teaching example, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(S)=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(N)=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(T=1\\mid S)=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(G=1\\mid S)=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(T=1\\mid N)=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(G=1\\mid N)=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith both alerts present, the unnormalised mass for S is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.096<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2\\cdot0.8\\cdot0.6=0.096<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile that for N is 0.016. Normalising gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.096<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.112<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(S\\mid T=1,G=1)=0.096/0.112=6/7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe illustration shows the calculation steps for both classes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Estimation, smoothing and numerical stability<\/h2>\n<p>Bernoulli Naive Bayes uses the presence and absence of a binary\nfeature; multinomial Naive Bayes is suitable for non-negative count\nfeatures such as word frequencies. Gaussian Naive Bayes models\ncontinuous features using class-specific normal distributions. For a\nbinary feature with m ones in n cases of a class, additive smoothing\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(m+\\alpha)/(n+2\\alpha)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus an event not observed so far does not immediately reduce the entire\nlikelihood to zero. The decision can be computed in log space:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_c=\\log P(c)+\\sum_j\\log P(x_j\\mid c)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNormalisation using log-sum-exp avoids numerical underflow when there\nare many features.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>log_score <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.log([<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>]) <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> np.log([[<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>]]).<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.exp(log_score <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> log_score.<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>())<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>posterior <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> w <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> w.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(posterior,[<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>In applications, the prior and feature parameters must be estimated\nfrom admissible training data. A change in the base rate during\noperation may also require reassessment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Calculate P(S) after a temperature alert with no noise alert.\nSolution: the masses are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.064<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2\\cdot0.8\\cdot0.4=0.064<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.064<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.8\\cdot0.1\\cdot0.8=0.064<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth classes receive 0.5. With smoothing using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand zero occurrences in eight cases, the estimated feature probability\nis 1/10.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/naive_bayes.html\">scikit-learn:\nNaive Bayes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/\">James et al.: An\nIntroduction to Statistical Learning with Applications in Python\n(2023)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M11-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M11-L3 – Decision trees and splitting criteria<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Bachelor: Classical learning algorithms (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate node impurity and\nsplit gain and justify tree depth and pruning.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decisions as a sequence of questions<\/h2>\n<p>A sorting model distinguishes quality classes A and B. A decision\ntree partitions the feature space by asking questions at internal nodes,\nfor example whether a measurement is at most 3.5. Each path to a leaf\ndescribes a subregion. The class prediction is based, for instance, on\nthe most frequent class in the leaf; for regression, the leaf mean can\nbe used. Trees can represent nonlinear boundaries and interactions\nwithout having to construct product features beforehand.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Gini impurity and gain<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001584_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Root with four A and two B, three A on the left, one A and two B on the right; Gini gain 2/9.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Root with four A and two B, three A on\nthe left, one A and two B on the right; Gini gain 2/9.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Decision tree: gain from a split.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For the class proportions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat a node, the Gini impurity is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/munder><msubsup><mi>p<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_G=1-\\sum_c p_c^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Alternatively, entropy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_H=-\\sum_c p_c\\log_2 p_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasures the degree of mixing (with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\log_2 0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nFor a binary split with n cases and child sizes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_l,n_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na greedy procedure maximises the local gain<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta I=I_{\\mathrm{parent}}-\\frac{n_l}{n}I_l-\\frac{n_r}{n}I_r.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The six training values 1 to 6 have the classes A,A,A,B,A,B. The\nparent node contains four A and two B, giving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_G=4/9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith the split\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>3.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\le3.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe left side has A=3 and B=0, and the right has A=1 and B=2. The\nimpurities are 0 and 4/9, and the gain is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4/9-2/9=2/9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe illustration visualises this split. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>4.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\le4.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe children are (3,1) and (1,1); the gain is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>36<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4/9-(4/6)(3/8)-(2/6)(1/2)=1/36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first of these two splits is better. A complete search step must\nalso examine the other admissible thresholds.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Tree growth and control<\/h2>\n<p>A single tree usually searches for locally optimal splits in\nsuccession; this does not guarantee a globally optimal tree. Too many\nsmall leaves can memorise random patterns. Maximum depth, minimum leaf\nsize and pruning constrain growth. Cost-complexity pruning uses a\ncriterion of the form\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(T)+\\alpha|\\mathcal L(T)|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nR is an empirical tree risk (depending on the implementation, for\nexample, the weighted leaf impurity), and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathcal L(T)|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the number of leaves. Larger\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npenalises complex trees more heavily. Thresholds must be selected using\ndevelopment data. Standardisation is not required for ordinary trees\nwith numerical thresholds; missing or categorical values require a\ndefined treatment.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.tree <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> DecisionTreeClassifier<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>]]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>model <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, random_state<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>).fit(X,y)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> model.tree_.threshold[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">3.5<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Also compare the threshold 5.5. The left side contains four A and one\nB, the right side one B. The gain is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>25<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>45<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.178<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4/9-(5/6)(8/25)=8/45\\approx0.178<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nless than 2/9. The tree of depth one selects 3.5. Why is a pure leaf\ncontaining only one case not yet proof of a reliable prediction? Because\nits empirical purity is based on very few observations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/tree.html\">scikit-learn:\nDecision Trees<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/\">James et al.: An\nIntroduction to Statistical Learning with Applications in Python\n(2023)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M11-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M11-L4 – Random forests and bagging<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Bachelor: Classical learning algorithms (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L7<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L5<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain bootstrap aggregation,\nfeature randomisation and the importance of correlated model errors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Many trees instead of a single split<\/h2>\n<p>Small changes in measurement data can alter an early branch of a\ndecision tree. This may change entire subtrees. Bagging (bootstrap\naggregating) trains several models on random samples drawn with\nreplacement from the training set and combines their outputs. The\ntraining runs can be executed independently; their data and subsequent\nerrors are not necessarily independent.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Variance and samples<\/h2>\n<p>If n cases are drawn uniformly n times with replacement, a particular\ncase is omitted with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-1/n)^n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\napproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.368<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-1}\\approx0.368<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor large n. Each base model therefore has training cases outside its\nbootstrap sample (out-of-bag, OOB). Predictions can be collected from\nthe models that have not seen the case under consideration. Such an\ninternal estimate does not replace an independent final evaluation,\nparticularly with group dependencies or temporal change.<\/p>\n<p>For B identically distributed numerical model predictions with\nvariance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand equal pairwise correlation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>B<\/mi><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>B<\/mi><\/munderover><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>B<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}\\left(\\frac1B\\sum_{b=1}^B Z_b\\right)=\\sigma^2\\left(\\rho+\\frac{1-\\rho}{B}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The formula follows by adding the B variances and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(B-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncovariances and dividing by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is 1.3. More models reduce the uncorrelated component; the\nremaining correlation limits the gain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Additional randomness in the forest<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001734_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Three bootstrap samples lead to trees with example random feature subsets per node; outputs are averaged.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three bootstrap samples lead to trees\nwith example random feature subsets per node; outputs are\naveraged.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Random forest: two sources of randomness.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A random forest typically restricts the features considered at each\nnode to a random subset. This encourages the trees to use different\nstructures. The illustration shows the separate randomisation of data\nand features. For regression, tree values are averaged; for\nclassification, majority voting or averaging class probabilities is\ncommon, depending on the implementation. Scikit-learn uses averaged tree\nprobabilities in its RandomForestClassifier. The number of trees\nstabilises the estimate, while depth, leaf size and feature selection\ncontrol the individual trees.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.datasets <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> make_classification<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.ensemble <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> RandomForestClassifier<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X,y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> make_classification(n_samples<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">300<\/span>,n_features<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                          n_informative<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,random_state<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>f <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>,max_features<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;sqrt&#39;<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                           oob_score<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>,random_state<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>).fit(X,y)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(f.estimators_) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">100<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> f.oob_score_ <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(f.oob_score_,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The example tests internal OOB evaluation on synthetic data, not\nsuitability for a real plant. Impurity-based feature importances can\nfavour features with many splitting possibilities; permutation\nimportance on independent data helps but also has limitations with\ncorrelated features.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>What is the variance with B=100 and unchanged\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nSolution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.03<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4(0.25+0.75/100)=1.03<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe limit as B tends to infinity is 1. Without correlation, the variance\nwould be 0.04.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/ensemble.html\">scikit-learn:\nEnsembles<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/\">James et al.: An\nIntroduction to Statistical Learning with Applications in Python\n(2023)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M11-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M11-L5 – Boosting and additive models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Bachelor: Classical learning algorithms (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive additive learning steps\nand explain the roles of learning rate, loss and weak base models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A model is improved step by step<\/h2>\n<p>While bagging trains many models separately, boosting builds a\nsequence of coordinated models. A new base model is intended to reduce\nthe errors or loss of the existing combination. The combination often\ntakes an additive form. For regression, a tree can provide small\ncorrections for different feature regions. This differs from\nindependently averaging many complete predictions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Gradient boosting with squared loss<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001531_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Four targets 2,4,6,8; initial value 5 is corrected to 4,4,6,6; MSE falls from 5 to 2.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four targets 2,4,6,8; initial value 5 is\ncorrected to 4,4,6,6; MSE falls from 5 to 2.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Gradient boosting: first correction.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(y,F)=\\tfrac12(y-F)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the current prediction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{m-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe negative gradient with respect to the prediction is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_i=y_i-F_{m-1}(x_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere it corresponds to the residual. A model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis fitted to these residuals. With step size\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis simple variant gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_m(x)=F_{m-1}(x)+\\eta h_m(x),\\qquad 0&lt;\\eta\\le1.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For four cases with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=(2,4,6,8)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe constant initial prediction is the mean of 5. The residuals are\n(-3,-1,1,3). A decision stump groups the first two and last two cases;\nits leaf means are -2 and 2. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_1=(4,4,6,6)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mean squared error falls from 5 to 2. The illustration tracks this\ncorrection. In the second step the residuals are (-2,0,0,2). An optimal\nstump isolates an endpoint case; the two symmetrical splits are\nequivalent. If it isolates the first case, the correction values are -2\nand 2/3; after a half step the new MSE is 1. Isolating the last case\nyields the same MSE.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Other losses and regularisation<\/h2>\n<p>With a different differentiable loss, the negative gradients are\ngenerally not simple residuals. Some methods subsequently optimise\nadditional step sizes or leaf values. For binary classification, a\nlogistic loss function can be used with an additive raw score; the\nlogistic transformation maps it to probabilities. AdaBoost is another\nboosting variant in which misclassified cases receive greater weight in\nsubsequent training steps. Both methods are sequential but not\nidentical. Small learning rates often require more steps; tree depth,\nsampling fraction and early stopping can constrain fitting. Outliers and\nincorrect labels deserve particular attention.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.ensemble <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> GradientBoostingRegressor<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>).reshape(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">6.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">8.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>m <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> GradientBoostingRegressor(n_estimators<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, max_depth<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                              learning_rate<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,random_state<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>).fit(X,y)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">list<\/span>(m.staged_predict(X))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(p[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(np.mean((y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The example shows training steps; a number of trees selected using\ndevelopment data is subsequently evaluated on independent data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>What would the first step look like with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nSolution: (3,3,7,7), MSE=1. The large step is faster in this example but\nneed not generalise better on more complex data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/ensemble.html\">scikit-learn:\nEnsembles<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/\">James et al.: An\nIntroduction to Statistical Learning with Applications in Python\n(2023)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M11-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M11-L6 – Support vector machines and the kernel principle<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Bachelor: Classical learning algorithms (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L4<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain the margin, hinge loss\nand kernels as inner products in a feature space.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Separation with a safety margin<\/h2>\n<p>With two classes and numerical features, a hyperplane\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w^\\top x+b=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan serve as a decision boundary. Let the binary labels be -1 and +1.\nRather than merely seeking any separating line, the linear support\nvector machine (SVM) favours a boundary that, for separable data, leaves\na wide gap between the nearest training cases of the two classes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Geometry and the soft margin<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001719_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two classes at x1=1 and x1=3; decision boundary x1=2 and margin width 2.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two classes at x1=1 and x1=3; decision\nboundary x1=2 and margin width 2.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Linear SVM: decision boundary and margin.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The normalisation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_i(w^\\top x_i+b)\\ge1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor all cases defines the margin planes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w^\\top x+b=\\pm1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe distance between them is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/\\|w\\|_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the negative-class points (1,1),(1,3) and positive-class points\n(3,1),(3,3),\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=(1,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngive an optimal linear separation. The boundary is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe margin planes at 1 and 3; all four cases lie on the margins. The\nillustration shows the geometry.<\/p>\n<p>For non-separable data, a soft-margin SVM allows margin violations. A\ncommon objective function is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"><\/mspace><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\min_{w,b}\\;\\tfrac12\\|w\\|_2^2+C\\sum_i\\max(0,1-y_i(w^\\top x_i+b)).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The second term contains the hinge loss. Larger C gives more weight\nto violations; smaller C tends to favour stronger regularisation. A\npoint on the correct side can still incur loss if it lies within the\nmargin. Support vectors determine the decision function in the dual\nformulation; other cases with a dual coefficient of zero make no direct\ncontribution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Nonlinear boundaries through kernels<\/h2>\n<p>A kernel\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K(x,z)=\\langle\\phi(x),\\phi(z)\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomputes an inner product in a feature space without always having to\ngenerate its coordinates explicitly. One example is the Gaussian RBF\nkernel\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K(x,z)=\\exp(-\\gamma\\|x-z\\|_2^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLarge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconfines similarity more strongly to the immediate neighbourhood. Not\nevery similarity function is a valid kernel; positive semidefiniteness\nof the Gram matrix is a central condition. Scaling, C and kernel\nparameters must be chosen appropriately; for very large datasets, a\nkernel SVM can require substantial time and memory. Raw scores are not\ncalibrated probabilities.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.svm <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> SVC<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>m <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> SVC(kernel<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;linear&#39;<\/span>, C<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>).fit(X,y)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(m.coef_,[[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(m.intercept_,[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> m.predict([[<span class=\"fl\">2.5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]])[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>For w and b as above, let a positive point be at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2.4;2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1=2.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts score is 0.4 and its hinge loss is 0.6. It is correctly classified\nbut lies within the margin. For two points with squared distance 4 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe RBF kernel value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.1353<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-2}\\approx0.1353<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/svm.html\">scikit-learn:\nSupport Vector Machines<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/\">James et al.: An\nIntroduction to Statistical Learning with Applications in Python\n(2023)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M11-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M11-L7 – Comparison, ensembles and error analysis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Bachelor: Classical learning algorithms (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L6<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L7<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L1<\/a>–<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare learning methods on\ncommon development data, justify ensembles and investigate groups of\nerrors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The appropriate model family depends on the task<\/h2>\n<p>When inspecting workpieces, we have several model families to choose\nfrom. k-NN uses local similarity, Naive Bayes simplified probability\nmodels, trees rule-like splits, forests averaged tree predictions,\nboosting successive corrections and SVMs regularised separating\nsurfaces. No method invariably wins regardless of data volume, features,\nerror costs, computational budget and data distribution. For comparison,\nwe use the same data split, the same target definition and a predefined\nmetric; preprocessing and hyperparameter selection remain within the\ndevelopment phase.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why different errors can be useful<\/h2>\n<p>In hard voting, the majority of class labels decides; in soft voting,\nthe largest averaged class probability decides. For M models and\nnormalised weights\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_m\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_m a_m=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe soft output is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>M<\/mi><\/munderover><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat p(c\\mid x)=\\sum_{m=1}^M a_m\\hat p_m(c\\mid x).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The probabilities should be comparably calibrated. Three models\noutput values of 0.9, 0.4 and 0.4 for class B. Hard voting with a\nthreshold of 0.5 yields A; equally weighted soft voting gives 1.7/3 and\ntherefore B. In stacking, a further model is trained on predictions\ninstead of using a fixed combination. Its training features must come\nfrom predictions for cases not used to train the respective base model\n(out-of-fold), so that it does not learn from overly optimistic training\nscores.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Diagnosis using specific errors<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001566_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Three models with two errors each; the majority is wrong only in case 2 and achieves seven out of eight correct predictions.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three models with two errors each; the\nmajority is wrong only in case 2 and achieves seven out of eight correct\npredictions.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Error overlap in majority voting.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>On a synthetic validation set of eight cases, models A, B and C have\nerrors at the following positions: A at 1 and 2, B at 2 and 3, C at 4\nand 5. All three achieve 6/8 correct predictions. In binary\nclassification, a majority decision is incorrect exactly when at least\ntwo models are incorrect; here this occurs only in case 2. The\nillustration shows the overlap. The ensemble achieves 7/8. However, an\nimprovement on these eight cases alone is not proof of general\nsuperiority.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.tile(y,(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> j, cases <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">enumerate<\/span>([[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>]]):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    p[j,cases] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> p[j,cases]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>((p <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> y).mean(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.75<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>majority <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (p.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>).astype(<span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (majority <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> y).mean() <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.875<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Errors are investigated by class, measurement range, device type and\ntime of collection. For small subgroups, absolute case counts and\nuncertainty are important. High errors in one group can arise from poor\nmeasurement, new data distributions, label problems or model\nlimitations; attributing them to a cause requires further evidence.\nModel selection, thresholds and ensemble weights are determined on\ndevelopment data. The held-out test set remains untouched until the\nfinal evaluation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>If all three models happened to be incorrect on cases 1 and 2, what\nadded value would hard voting offer on these data? Solution: None; the\nsame two cases remain incorrect. In addition to individual quality,\ndifferences between errors are crucial. In the soft-voting exercise\nabove, weights (0.2; 0.4; 0.4) give a B proportion of 0.5; a predefined\nrule is needed to handle ties.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/ensemble.html\">scikit-learn:\nEnsembles<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/\">James et al.: An\nIntroduction to Statistical Learning with Applications in Python\n(2023)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
